          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much space do I need for a budget home gym?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A full barbell setup needs about 8x8 feet of clear floor plus at least 8 feet of ceiling height for overhead pressing, and 9 feet if you plan to do any overhead work standing on a raised platform. A dumbbell-and-bench setup works in roughly 6x6 feet. Kettlebell and band training needs about 20 square feet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it cheaper to build a home gym or pay for a membership?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "At a typical $40-60 monthly membership, a $1,400 budget home gym pays for itself in roughly two to three years, and most of the equipment holds significant resale value afterward. Cast iron plates, barbells, and racks depreciate very little, so the true cost of ownership is often far lower than the purchase price suggests.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Where is it safe to save, and where is it not?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Save on plates, flooring, dumbbells, and accessories — cast iron is cast iron, and horse stall mats are identical to gym flooring at a third the price. Do not save on the power rack, the safety pins, or the bench frame, since those are the only pieces whose failure can injure you. A budget rack from a reputable brand is fine; an unbranded rack with no published steel gauge or weight rating is not.",
