        <!-- SECTION 1: What It Costs -->
        <div id="what-it-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Budget Home Gym Actually Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single biggest barrier to building a home gym is not money — it's not knowing what the real number is. Fitness marketing tends to show either a $6,000 all-in-one machine or a $30 yoga mat, with nothing in between, and the space between those two extremes is where nearly every worthwhile home gym actually lives. So before any product recommendation, here are the honest numbers we arrived at after pricing every component and buying most of them ourselves.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>complete barbell gym</strong> — meaning a power rack with safety pins, a 7-foot Olympic bar, roughly 300 lb of cast iron plates, an adjustable bench, and rubber flooring — lands between $1,200 and $1,600 new in 2026. That setup lets you squat, bench, deadlift, overhead press, row, and do pull-ups, which is every fundamental barbell movement a strength program is built from. Nothing you add after that changes what you're capable of training; it only changes convenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>dumbbell-and-bench gym</strong> costs $400 to $600 and covers a genuinely large percentage of the same training. Adjustable dumbbells and an adjustable bench give you pressing at every angle, rows, lunges, split squats, curls, extensions, and carries. What you lose is the ability to load heavy — a 52.5 lb per-hand ceiling caps what you can do for legs and back long before it caps what you can do for chest and shoulders.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>minimalist setup</strong> — one kettlebell in the 35-45 lb range plus a stackable resistance band set — costs under $150 and occupies about twenty square feet. This is not a consolation prize. Swings, goblet squats, presses, rows, and carries done consistently with one bell will build more strength and conditioning than a fully equipped gym used twice a month, which is the actual fate of most home gym purchases.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Budget Home Gym by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1,400</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Complete barbell gym, new</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$150</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Kettlebell + bands starter</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8x8 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Floor space a barbell setup needs</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">~2.5 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Payback vs a $50/mo membership</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The membership comparison is worth sitting with, because it changes how the purchase should feel. At a fairly typical $50 per month, a $1,400 gym pays for itself in about two and a half years. But that calculation understates the case badly, because it treats the equipment as consumed. It isn't. Cast iron plates, Olympic barbells, kettlebells, and steel racks are among the slowest-depreciating consumer goods there are — a well-kept rack and plate set routinely resells for 60-80% of its purchase price years later. Factor resale in and the effective cost of a home gym is often closer to eighteen months of membership fees, plus you never drive anywhere again.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Build Order -->
        <div id="build-order" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Build Order: What to Buy First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost nobody buys a home gym in one purchase, and the order matters more than most people expect — partly because some items are painful to add after the fact, and partly because buying in the wrong order is how people end up spending money twice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: The Floor</h3>
          <p>
            Flooring goes first, always, and this is the step people skip. Once a rack is assembled and three hundred pounds of plates are stacked on it, laying mats underneath means disassembling everything. Beyond the logistics, an unprotected concrete slab is genuinely vulnerable: a dropped 45 lb plate will chip and spall bare concrete, and the damage spreads from there. If the slab is going to get a coating at all, that happens before the mats — our <a href="/how-to-apply-garage-floor-epoxy/">guide to applying garage floor epoxy</a> covers the prep and cure timeline, and it needs to be fully cured before anything heavy lands on it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: The Rack</h3>
          <p>
            The rack determines what your gym can do and where everything else lives, so it's the second decision. It's also the piece with the least flexibility later — its footprint, its height, and whether it's bolted down all constrain the room around it. Buy the rack before the bar and plates, even if it means training with bodyweight for a month while you save for the rest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Bar and Plates</h3>
          <p>
            A bar and 300 lb of plates is the standard starting load, and it's enough for the overwhelming majority of home lifters for years. Buy the bar new and the plates used if you can find them — more on that below. Resist the urge to buy bumper plates on a budget; they cost roughly three times what cast iron costs per pound and their advantage only matters if you're dropping weights from overhead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: The Bench</h3>
          <p>
            An adjustable bench comes fourth because a flat bench alone is enough to start pressing, and because bench prices swing wildly on sale. Waiting a few weeks for a discount on an adjustable bench is a better use of patience than almost anywhere else in this list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Everything Else</h3>
          <p>
            Dumbbells, kettlebells, bands, a cardio machine, and accessories all come after the barbell setup is complete, because none of them expand what you can train as much as the first four steps do. The exception is the person who knows for certain they'll never barbell train, in which case adjustable dumbbells and a bench become steps one and two.
          </p>
        </div>

