        <div id="save-vs-splurge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Where to Save and Where Not To
          </h2>

          <p>
            Building a gym on a budget is not about buying the cheapest version of everything. It's about knowing which items are commodities, where a cheap version and an expensive version are functionally identical, and which items are the ones holding a loaded barbell over your throat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Save Freely On These</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Plates.</strong> Cast iron is cast iron. A 45 lb plate from a budget brand and a 45 lb plate from a premium brand weigh the same, load the same, and last the same forever. The only meaningful differences are finish quality and how accurately the stated weight matches actual weight, and for home training a two percent variance is irrelevant. Buy the cheapest cast iron you can find per pound, and buy it used if possible.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Flooring.</strong> As covered above, horse stall mats and gym flooring are the same recycled rubber sold to different customers at very different prices. This is the single easiest few hundred dollars to save in a home gym build.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Accessories.</strong> Jump ropes, ab wheels, resistance bands, foam rollers, dip belts, and lifting straps have essentially no premium version worth paying for at home. The failure mode of a cheap ab wheel is that it breaks and you buy another one for eleven dollars.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cardio, mostly.</strong> A $300 magnetic rower does not feel like a $1,000 air rower, but it delivers the same physiological stimulus if you work equally hard on it. Cardio equipment is where the price-to-benefit curve flattens fastest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Save On These</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>The rack and its safeties.</strong> This is the only item on the list whose failure can seriously injure you. That does not mean buying an expensive rack — the Titan T-3 in this guide is a budget rack — it means buying from a manufacturer that publishes steel gauge, tube dimensions, and a rated capacity, and refusing to buy from one that doesn't. An unbranded rack with no published specs is not a bargain; it's an unknown holding a barbell above your neck.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The bench frame.</strong> Not the padding, the frame. A bench that shifts laterally under a heavy press is genuinely dangerous, and the difference between a stable bench and an unstable one is base geometry rather than price. Look for a wide, triangulated base and treat any bench built on a single narrow post as unsuitable for heavy work.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The bar, within reason.</strong> You don't need a $500 competition bar, but you should avoid the bottom of the market. A bar with no published tensile strength rating, a chrome-over-steel finish, and welded rather than rotating sleeves is a bar that can bend permanently under a load you'll reach within a year.
          </p>

